{"hash":"e313235d","configHash":"b69fd196215c","embedModel":"bge-m3","dims":1024,"matchTau":0.65,"entries":[{"q":"What is this documentation about?","qnorm":"what is this documentation about","lang":"en","vec":"","ids":["concepts/guarantees#claims-this-documentation-will-not-make","guide/comparison#","reference/config#product","guide/comparison#sources","guide/comparison#what-reaches-the-reader"],"gate":{"pass":true,"G":0.6894816789633577,"D":0.5859755719511436,"L":1,"z":0.5571951143902287,"n":476,"channel":"raw","threshold":0.3,"mode":"hybrid"},"answer":{"lang":"en","text":"This documentation explains how to manage a system that routes requests across multiple models, handles rate limits, and optimizes resource usage. It covers:\n\n1. **Free tier limitations** [1] - Four scenarios where model rotation stops (e.g., 401 errors, daily limits) and how the system avoids redundant requests. Retries occur via a 'pool' that never drops models, ensuring availability.\n\n2. **Turn mechanics** [2] - How a 'primed turn' preloads evidence into the first message, reducing steps. If failed, it cascades to other services in the 'answer ladder' until resolved.\n\n3. **Configuration** [3] - The `chat.chain` setting defines service order (e.g., 'auto' selects billed accounts first). Environments with multiple keys route requests across all available providers.\n\n4. **Evaluation & calibration** [4] - Processes to balance refusal thresholds. Feedback from users shapes thresholds, avoiding over-refusal while measuring baseline performance. Calibration data is embedded in builds.\n\nThe guide emphasizes avoiding pitfalls like redundant retries, ensuring model availability, and aligning system behavior with user feedback.","citations":["guide/free-tier#four-failures-that-do-not-rotate","concepts/a-turn#4-the-primed-turn","reference/config#chatchain","guide/evaluation#what-it-needs~2"],"confidence":1,"promptHash":"df00ecbc","model":"qwen3:8b"},"answerAttempt":null},{"q":"How do I get started?","qnorm":"how do i get started","lang":"en","vec":"","ids":["guide#overview~3","guide#overview~2","reference/cli#level","guide/providers#two-keys-and-which-answers-first","install/react#keeping-it-in-step"],"gate":{"pass":true,"G":0.6852966705933413,"D":0.5803955607911218,"L":1,"z":0.5560791121582244,"n":476,"channel":"raw","threshold":0.3,"mode":"hybrid"},"answer":{"lang":"en","text":"To get started with DocPilot, first ensure your configuration is correct by running `npx docpilot doctor --proxy` to validate setup and check for missing components. This command confirms the panel will render or lists what is missing, exiting non-zero if not ready—useful for CI integration. Next, configure the UI by setting `ui.trigger` in your config file. A list of placements (e.g., `'nav'`, `'fab'`) determines where the trigger appears. For example, `ui: { trigger: 'fab' }` sets the floating button as default. Also, handle translations by defining keys in your i18n setup, ensuring terms are canonical or aliases to avoid rewrite cycles. Refer to the configuration guide for detailed options.","citations":["guide/evaluation#bench-two-retrieval-configurations~2","reference/config#ui~2","guide/i18n#the-keys~3","reference/config#it-rewrites-it-never-adds"],"confidence":1,"promptHash":"df00ecbc","model":"qwen3:8b"},"answerAttempt":null},{"q":"How do I authenticate requests?","qnorm":"how do i authenticate requests","lang":"en","vec":"","ids":["guide/evaluation#what-it-needs","guide/panel#a-question-with-a-key-in-it","reference/config#chatbaseurl","concepts/a-turn#1-the-credential-check","guide/social-openers#what-it-deliberately-does-not-do","guide/panel#hello","concepts/a-turn#2-is-it-a-question-at-all"],"gate":{"pass":true,"G":1,"D":1,"L":1,"z":0.6402132804265609,"n":476,"channel":"raw","threshold":0.3,"mode":"hybrid"},"answer":null,"answerAttempt":{"promptHash":"df00ecbc","model":"qwen3:8b"}}]}